One of the fundamental assumptions in cheminformatics is that two structurally similar compounds display similar chemical properties \citep{Johnson1990}. While there are important exceptions, namely activity cliffs \citep{Stumpfe2012, vanTilborg2022}, this is a well-established concept. However, there is no single way to define similarity between compounds, and this is where the assumption begins to break down in practice. When defining similarity between two molecules, we typically rely on some pairwise mathematical function, or coefficient, that takes in two molecular representations as input. There's two varying factors here, the representation and the coefficient. \cite{Bajusz2015} found that amongst standard coefficient, the choice between them matters little. This question questions the underlying representation. 

General-purpose molecular representations are designed to work across chemical domains. Consequently, when considering data from a specific domain, many of their features show little to no variance. Some representations assign separate molecules identical feature vectors. Since common fingerprints do not encode stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}, they are often unable to differentiate stereoisomers, which can have substantially different measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}. As these coefficients compare every feature, irrelevant features dilute the result. Different representations also disagree about which molecules are close based on the information they carry. 

