The problem of whether neighboring molecules can predict chemical activity has been measure before. The MODelability Index (MODI) of \citet{Golbraikh2014} scores a binary dataset by the proportion of nearest-neighbour pairs that share an activity class, producing a single value for how strongly the similarity assumption holds on that particular combination of representation and data. It is not clear whether a low value is due to the data being intrinsically hard to model or to the representation not capturing adequate features for the task. Other work in this area relates to activity cliffs. \citet{Sheridan2020} compared experimental error, activity distribution and activity-cliff metrics as predictors of how well a dataset can be modeled, and found the cliff metrics the strongest of the three. However, they found that these metrics cannot separate real cliffs from  those produced by uncertain activity measurements. \citet{MedinaFranco2013} point out that some of these activity cliffs are artifacts of the chosen representation, similarity threshold, or noise in the data. Activity cliffs are distrinct from noise, as they carry some of the most valuable information about the interaction between structure and activity. None of these measures ask specifically about whether the specific features within these representations matter for the property at hand. 

