\subsection{Molecules the representation cannot tell apart}
% ---- NOTES: Molecules the representation cannot tell apart ----
% Takeaway: some representations give two different molecules exactly the same vector, mostly stereoisomers.
% Takeaway: every model must then give both one prediction. More data, tuning or a better model cannot remove that error; only a change of representation can.
%
% Paragraph 1: the flaw, and why no model can fix it.
% - A standard representation can give two different molecules exactly the same feature vector.
% - Two molecules with one vector must receive one prediction from every model.
% - If their measured labels differ, part of the error is fixed before any model is trained.
% - More data, more tuning or a better model in the same representation cannot remove this error. Only a change of representation can.

A standard fingerprint- or descriptor-based representation can produce the same feature vector for two separate molecules. In turn, models using this representation will produce a single prediction for the molecules with identical features. It should be noted that identical feature vectors within a dataset do not necessarily mean that they derive from the same underlying molecule. The dataset could just hold two different measurements of the same molecule twice, as is possible with data pooled across different sources.

%
% Paragraph 2: three reasons two rows can be identical, counted separately.
