% - Featurisation failed and returned a row of zeros. This is a pipeline fault.
% - Two different molecules that the representation cannot separate. Only this case is a flaw of the representation.
% - Within the third case, count separately the molecules that differ only in stereochemistry.
%
% Paragraph 3: why stereoisomers tie, and why learned models are not immune.
% - Most common fingerprints do not encode stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}.
% - The Morgan fingerprints used here are built with chirality off, the library default.
% - The 2D descriptor sets (PDV, Mordred) also ignore stereochemistry. On every dataset where they were counted, each molecule they tie is a stereoisomer of another.
% - Stereoisomers can differ in measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}. A stereo-blind representation forces one prediction onto molecules with different measured values.
% - Message-passing networks are bounded by the 1-Weisfeiler-Lehman test (Chapter 2). So some non-isomorphic graphs receive the same vector \citep{Xu2019, morris2019wl}, \citep[pp.~35--38]{Dablander2024}.
% - The Weisfeiler-Lehman graph-kernel features \citep{Shervashidze2011} tie 105 of 1,412 hERG Ki molecules. 56 of these are stereo-only. The other 49 differ in more than stereochemistry (preliminary).
% - Learned embeddings are not immune. SMI-TED, a pretrained SMILES encoder, ties 52 hERG Ki, 633 LogD and 177 Caco-2 molecules, all stereo-only (preliminary).
% - A tokeniser can drop stereochemistry before the model sees the string. The ChemBERTa-77M-MTR tokeniser used in Chapter 3 gives 72 of 1,415 hERG Ki molecules the same token sequence as another molecule.
% - None of the 72 were one string before tokenising. The largest merged groups are stereoisomers whose stereo markers the tokeniser does not keep.
%

Many fingerprints are computed without stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}. In this research, we build ECFP4 fingerprints with stereochemistry off while the 2D descriptor sets (PDV, Mordred) also ignore it such that for every dataset, each molecule they label a tie is a stereoisomer of another. Any representation read from the 2D graph merges stereoisomers, since the two share an identical graph. Separately, methods using the Weisfeiler--Lehman relabeling scheme are bounded by the 1-Weisfeiler--Lehman test explained in Chapter~\ref{chap:background}, so some non-isomorphic graphs also receive the same vector; the graph-kernel features tie 105 of 1,412 hERG $K_i$ molecules, 56 of which differ only in stereochemistry and 49 in more than that. Whether a learned embedding differentiates stereoisomers is up to its individual implementation. SMI-TED, a pretrained SMILES encoder, ties 52 hERG $K_i$, 633 LogD and 177 Caco-2 molecules, all stereoisomers. A tokenizer can drop stereochemistry before the model even sees the input string. The ChemBERTa-77M-MTR tokenizer used in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} gives 72 of 1,415 hERG $K_i$ molecules the same token sequence as another molecule. If the difference between stereoisomers was negligible, this wouldn't matter. However, stereoisomers can differ in measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}, meaning these representations are all lacking potentially important chemical information.

