The standard way of comparing two small molecules is the Tanimoto coefficient, which operates on any bit vector, applied to Morgan fingerprints \citep{LopezPerez2024}. Independently, the Tanimoto coefficient is described as the ``coefficient of choice'' for molecular similarity \citep{Bajusz2015}, while Morgan fingerprint, represented as ECFP4 in this research, is likewise one of the strongest fingerprints for the task, ranking amongst the strongest fingerprint for virtual screening and target prediction \citep{Capecchi2020}. ECFP4 and ECFP6 specifically, alongside topological torsion, rank among the best of 28 fingerprints tested for ordering diverse structures by similarity \citep{OBoyle2016}. However, it's not a one-size-fits-all solution. Across 96,456 human metabolites, 72.9\% had an ECFP4 nearest neighbour at Tanimoto distance 0 \citep{Capecchi2020}, again running into the issue of the representation lacking stereochemical information and more generally being unable to distinguish molecules. 

